.\" Not v7's page: this ls is 4.2BSD's, by way of RetroBSD.  See BESM-6 NOTES.
.TH LS 1
.SH NAME
ls  \-  list contents of directory
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ls
[
.B \-1ACFRacdfgilqrstu
] name ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
For each directory argument,
.I ls
lists the contents of the directory;
for each file argument,
.I ls
repeats its name and any other information requested.
The output is sorted alphabetically by default.
When no argument is given, the current directory is listed.
When several arguments are given,
the arguments are first sorted appropriately,
but file arguments appear
before directories and their contents.
.PP
By default, when the standard output is a terminal, the names are printed in as
many columns as will fit and unprintable characters are shown as
.RB ` ? ';
when it is not, one name is printed per line and every byte is printed as it is.
.RB ` \-C ',
.B \-1
and
.B \-q
ask for those forms explicitly.
.PP
There are several options:
.TP
.B  \-l
List in long format, giving mode, number of links, owner,
size in bytes, and time of last modification
for each file.
(See below.)
If the file is a special file the size field will instead contain
the major and minor device numbers.
.TP
.B  \-C
Force multi-column output; this is the default when the output is a terminal.
.TP
.B  \-1
Force one entry per line; this is the default when the output is not a terminal.
.B \-1 ,
.B \-C
and
.B \-l
each turn the other two off, so that shell aliasing works.
.TP
.B  \-F
Mark directories with a trailing
.RB ` / '
and executable files with a trailing
.RB ` * '.
.TP
.B  \-R
Recursively list subdirectories encountered.
.TP
.B  \-a
List all entries; usually
.RB ` . '
and
.RB ` .. '
are suppressed.
.TP
.B  \-A
List all entries except
.RB ` . '
and
.RB ` .. '.
It is the default for the super-user.
.TP
.B  \-q
Print each non-graphic character in a file name as
.RB ` ? ';
this is the default when the output is a terminal.
.TP
.B  \-t
Sort by time modified (latest first) instead of
by name, as is normal.
.TP
.B  \-s
Give size in blocks,
including indirect blocks, for each entry.
.TP
.B  \-d
If argument is a directory, list only its name, not
its contents (mostly used with
.B \-l
to get status
on directory).
.TP
.B  \-r
Reverse the order of sort to get reverse alphabetic
or oldest first as appropriate.
.TP
.B  \-u
Use time of last access instead of last
modification for sorting
.RB ( \-t )
or printing
.RB ( \-l ).
.TP
.B \-c
Use time of last modification to inode (mode, etc.)
instead of last modification to file for sorting
.RB ( \-t )
or printing
.RB ( \-l ).
.TP
.B  \-i
Print i-number in first column
of the report for each file listed.
.TP
.B  \-f
Force each argument to be interpreted as a directory
and list the name found in each slot.
This option turns off
.B "\-l, \-t, \-s,"
and
.B \-r,
and
turns on
.B \-a;
the order is the order in which entries
appear in the directory.
.TP
.B  \-g
Give group ID instead of owner ID in long listing.
.TP
.B  "\-L, \-o"
Accepted and ignored.  See
.B "OPTIONS THAT DO NOTHING HERE"
below.
.PP
The mode printed under the
.B \-l
option contains 11 characters
which are interpreted
as follows:
the first character is
.TP 3
.B  d
if the entry is a directory;
.br
.ns
.TP 3
.B  b
if the entry is a block-type special file;
.br
.ns
.TP 3
.B  c
if the entry is a character-type special file;
.br
.ns
.TP 3
.B  \-
if the entry is a plain file.
.PP
The next 9 characters are interpreted
as three sets of three bits each.
The first set refers to owner permissions;
the next to permissions to others in the same user-group;
and the last to all others.
Within each set the three characters indicate
permission respectively to read, to write, or to
execute the file as a program.
For a directory, `execute' permission is interpreted
to mean permission to search the directory
for a specified file.
The permissions are indicated as follows:
.TP 3
.B  r
if the file is readable;
.br
.ns
.TP 3
.B  w
if the file is writable;
.br
.ns
.TP 3
.B  x
if the file is executable;
.br
.ns
.TP 3
.B  \-
if the indicated permission is not granted.
.PP
The group-execute permission character is given
as
.B s
if the file has set-group-ID mode;
likewise the user-execute permission character is given
as
.B s
if the file has set-user-ID mode.
If the set-ID bit is on but the corresponding execute bit is not, the character
is
.B S
instead.
.PP
The last character of the mode (normally `x' or `\-') is
.B t
if the 1000 bit of the mode is on, or
.B T
if that bit is on and the execute bit is not.
See
.IR  chmod (1)
for the meaning of this mode.
.PP
When the sizes of the files in a directory
are listed, a total count of blocks,
including indirect blocks is printed.
.SH "OPTIONS THAT DO NOTHING HERE"
.B "Note:"
.B \-L
and
.B \-o
are parsed, so that a command line written for a 4.xBSD
.I ls
runs here rather than failing, and then nothing reads them.
.PP
.B \-L
asks that a symbolic link be followed rather than described.
.B This system has no symbolic links,
so every
.I stat
already behaves as
.B \-L
asks and there is nothing for the option to change.  Nothing is ever listed with
a leading
.RB ` l ',
and
.B \-F
has no
.RB ` @ '
to print.
.PP
.B \-o
asks for the 4.4BSD file-flags column \-
.IR uappnd ", " uchg ", " schg
and their fellows.
.B This system has no such flags:
.I struct stat
has no
.I st_flags
and there is no
.IR chflags (2)
to set one.
.SH "COLUMNS ARE COUNTED IN BYTES"
.B "Note:"
this system carries eight bits everywhere \- the console, the clists, the
filesystem and
.IR sh (1)
are all byte-transparent \- so a file name may hold Cyrillic.
.I Ls
counts a column's width in
.B bytes,
not in letters, so a name written in a multi-byte encoding is charged for its
bytes and a column holding one will be wider than it looks.
.PP
.B \-q
is unaffected by this and is deliberately narrower than 4.xBSD's: it replaces the
control characters and
.B rubout
only, where 4.xBSD replaced every byte above
.BR 0177 .
Since
.B \-q
is on by default at a terminal, the wider test would have turned every Cyrillic
name into a row of question marks.
.SH "BLOCKS ARE 1024 BYTES"
.B "Note:"
the block
.B \-s
and the
.B total
line count in is
.B 1024
bytes.
It is not this machine's filesystem block, which is
.B 3072
bytes \- 512 words of 48 bits \- and is three of them.
.PP
So every size
.B \-s
prints is a
.B multiple of three,
the smallest thing that can be allocated being one 3072-byte block: a one-byte file costs
three.
And the numbers are
.B half
what a PDP\-11 printed, v7 having counted in 512-byte blocks.
.PP
.IR Df (1M),
.IR du (1)
and
.IR quot (1M)
report in the same block.
.SH "BESM-6 NOTES"
The screen width cannot be asked for: this kernel's terminal calls are v7's and
there is no
.B TIOCGWINSZ.
.I Ls
uses 80 columns, or the value of
.B $COLUMNS
when that names a positive number.
.PP
There is no
.IR st_blocks ,
so the block counts are computed from the file's size and an entirely sparse file
is charged for the blocks it does not have.
.PP
.I Ls
is the first program here to read a directory through
.IR directory (3)
rather than by hand.
.SH FILES
/etc/passwd to get user ID's for
`ls \-l'.
.br
/etc/group to get group ID's for
`ls \-g'.
.SH SEE ALSO
directory(3), dir(5), chmod(1), find(1)
